foreign  [Music]  the purpose of this film is to show how  to record and code observations of  nursing personnel activities in  hospitals  the technique is described in recent  studies developed by the public health  service  the study method  has been published in the manual how to  study nursing activities in a patient  unit  other manuals how to study supervisor  activities in a hospital nursing service  and how to study the nursing service of  an outpatient department  describe adaptation  of this method of observation  the observer is the key figure in all  studies of nursing activities  she wears a white coat over her street  clothes  she does not wear a nurse's uniform or  cat  although she is one of the nurses  selected from among the nursing  personnel of the hospital  she is not dressed as a nurse so that  patients won't expect her to provide  care  the observer carries a pencil and a  clipboard  to which are attached a watch and  several blank observers record forms  here is the unit in which the  observations are to be made  and these are the people our observer  will study  we are going to demonstrate the work  sampling method of observation  we do not watch each member of the  nursing staff constantly but look to see  what she is doing during a selected  interval of time  in this study the interval is 15 minutes  the personnel who are to be observed  are tagged with cards which identify  them  according to their job title  rn for professional nurse  hn for head nurse and so on  when the unit employs more than one  person in each category  of nursing service  aides  practical nurses  professional nurses for example  the tags are numbered to help the  observer recognize  which individual she is observing  all members of the nursing service in  the study unit are tagged in this way  for the purpose of this film  our observer will locate and record  the activities of each staff member  once every 15 minutes  the head nurse  the professional nurse  in this study there are two  rn1  and rn2  the student nurse  the practical nurse  if more than one practical nurse is  employed on the unit they would be  tagged  pn1  pn2  and so on  then  the nursing aid is observed  the clerk  and any other personnel on the unit  employed by nursing service  the observer must find each person  assigned to nursing service  on the study unit  she must observe the activity being  performed at that time  then she records it  if there are more than 12 persons on  duty at any time during the study period  two observers will have to be assigned  four forms are needed for each hour of  observation  one form for every 15 minutes  here we see a simplified record  before the observation starts  the observer should fill in the headings  on all the forms she is going to use  begin with the name of the unit  then  the name of the observer  and the date  then  insert the time of day  when the observations are to be made  number the page  now list in column b  on each form  the code titles of the personnel to be  observed on this unit  the symbols  h n  r n  p n  s n  and so forth  are what we call the code titles for  each category of nursing personnel  now fill in  in column a  the exact time  when the study is to begin  remember  each member of the nursing staff must be  observed once  every 15 minutes  now we are ready to begin  the observer does not try to locate the  staff in the order listed on the forms  she observes the person nearest her  and records exactly what is happening  in column e on the form  the observer sees an aid wheeling a  patient down the hall  she asks the aide where she is going and  why  the aide replies i'm taking the patient  to x-ray  the observer immediately records the  activity  otherwise she might forget it  in the rush of each 15-minute  observation period  this activity is recorded in column e  as taking patient to x-ray  the activity observed  must be written clearly and concisely  so that any reader will know what  happened  to whom  and with whom  then it is coded according to areas  in column c  taking patient to x-ray  is an activity in the presence of a  patient  but it is not a treatment  therefore it is recorded in column c  as code 12  which you will recall from your list of  code references  is  other direct activities  the activity is now recorded  according to the level  of skill  which is required to perform it  accompanying a patient to x-ray  or to other departments of the hospital  does not usually require nursing aid  skills  so  enter  m e s  messenger  in column d  here is an example of another activity  observed  the head nurse is giving a student nurse  instructions  regarding the care of a specific patient  write this activity  in column e  then  in column c  area  code the activity as 13  because it is exchange of information  about a patient  next  insert in column d  the level of the activity being  performed  the level is hn  as this activity of the head nurse  is at her own level of responsibility  now let's assume we're recording the  same activity  but in relation to the student nurse  who is receiving the instruction  the activity is filled in  in column e  receiving assignment from head nurse  then  insert sn  in column d  as this is a proper level of activity  for a student nurse  in column c area  write 13 again  as this is also an exchange of  information about the patient  our next example  shows a clerk making out medicine cards  from the order book write this activity  in column e  in column c  insert  14  indirect care  now code the activity by level  cl  for clerk  copying information or other data  involves clerical skills  it is not in nursing activity  the observer next sees the practical  nurse  going into the corridor  and asks her where she is going  the practical nurse replies  i'm going to ask the head nurse how to  prepare an oxygen tent  for use on the unit  this activity will be filled in  in column e  then it is to be coded in column c  this code area is 21  in-service development  this is the chosen code because the  observer understands that what the  practical nurse is about to do  is something which will improve her  skill  in caring for patients  the level  which is pn  is then inserted in column d  the practical nurse  is functioning at her level  when receiving instructions from the  head nurse  here is our last example  the observer sees rn1  carrying a treatment tray as she leaves  the patient  she has just changed addressing  the activity is part of the care given  so it is recorded in column e  as changing patients dressing  it is coded by area in column c  as 11 giving care  encoded by level  rn  in column d  now let's review  the important steps to remember are  make only one observation  of each person  every 15 minutes  don't try to locate the staff in the  order listed  but just as you happen to find them  first  write the activity clearly and concisely  then code it by area  then by level  the observer now waits until the next  15-minute interval is to begin  before making any additional observation  because her 15 minutes aren't up she  relaxes  [Music]  um  [Music]  this is the second part of a two-part  film demonstrating observation  techniques described in the division of  nursing resources manual how to study  nursing activities in a patient unit  [Music]  in part one we observed the activities  of various kinds of nursing personnel  we recorded our observations on the  observer's record sheet  entering the activity clearly and  concisely  coding it by area  and by level  and making only one observation  of each person  every 15 minutes  we will continue to use the same  observation method  and the same 15-minute observation  period in this film  however this time we will talk about  activities which the observer may find  confusing or difficult to code  because what the person observed is  doing is not evident  several persons are observed  simultaneously  the activity observed apparently is not  at the level of the person performing it  one person is performing two activities  at the same time  or perhaps the activity involves a  telephone conversation with the observer  hearing only part of the discussion  let us first take a situation where the  purpose of an activity by a member of  the nursing staff is not obvious  to record the activity as walking down  hall would not really identify the  activity  what the observer must learn is intent  why this activity is being performed  what are you doing the observer asks  coming from pharmacy the nurse might  reply  this answer is still inadequate  why did you go to pharmacy to get unit  drugs  the observer now has enough information  to record the activity  she enters 30 after rn in the area  column because the activity is  housekeeping  and even though the person observed is a  nurse carrying medicines  the level of the activity is messenger  a student nurse walking down the hall  volunteers i'm answering the light in  407.  this activity is indirect care  the observer codes it as area 14.  and under level enters sn  what are you doing the observer asks the  practical nurse  i'm waiting for the diet trays to arrive  from the kitchen  this again is indirect care  it is a dietary level  a student nurse tells the observer  i'm waiting to demonstrate that i know  how to do an irrigation  this is coded area 22 student education  the level is sn  the head nurse seems to be preoccupied  the observer asks what are you thinking  about  the head nurse answers that she is  worried about a patient's reaction to  medication  this is indirect care  and since reaction to medicine is  properly a head nurse concern it is  coded hn in the level column  suppose the head nurse had answered  i was thinking about a movie i saw last  night  this would be a strictly personal  thought having no bearing on the work  situation and would be so coded  often several people are observed  simultaneously  the observer records each individual's  activity separately  here the rn is assigning duties to a  student nurse and a nursing aide  the observer records the rn's activity  as exchange of information about patient  the student nurse asks should i give the  treatment before or after bathing the  patient  the observer records this as asking how  to assure best results from treatment  she codes it area 22  because it contributes to the  educational development of a student  the nursing aide has been listening to  this exchange of information  her activity is coded area 13  at n a level  when an activity is not at the level of  the person performing it the observer  must learn the reason behind it the head  nurse is arranging flowers at a  patient's bedside  as she leaves the patient's room the  observer questions her  i hate to see flowers just stuck in a  vase  in this instance the activity is  housekeeping  even though it was performed by a head  nurse  had she replied i wanted to see how the  patient was doing the intention would  have been entirely different  the level then would be hn  whenever two activities are being  performed at the same time by one person  the observer must decide which is more  important  an rn is passing diet trades  she pauses when a patient asks her about  his diet  explaining dietary needs to a patient is  an activity that outranks the passing of  trays  in such a situation the observer records  the higher level activity  and instead of writing d for dietary  under level she writes rn  as this activity is not a specific  treatment it is coded in area 12.  if two activities being performed by the  same person are at equal level  as when an rn prepares a piece of  equipment for a treatment and at the  same time demonstrates this procedure to  a student nurse  the observer lists both activities  she leaves the area column blank until  she can ask the study director which  activity is more important for the  purpose of this study  telephone conversations present a  special problem because the observer  cannot hear everything that is said  the observer waits until the call has  been completed and then asks her  questions  she must learn the purpose and content  of the conversation and to whom the  nurse was speaking  only then can she code the area and  level of the activity accurately  many other situations are confusing or  difficult to code meal time is an  example  if a full-time nurse takes more time  than authorized for meals  the excess time is recorded personal  meal time for part-time employees is  always coded personal  sometimes a nurse cannot be located  within the observation period  the observer simply waits until the next  time she sees her then asks her what she  was doing and enters the activity  accordingly  you have seen examples of typical  problems which you as an observer will  encounter  other situations will undoubtedly arise  their solution  [Music]  and the success of your nursing activity  study  will depend upon your ability to  function as a keen and interested  observer  you

